# Project: ETH: Year One
Undertale-style top-down RPG (overworld) + Cuphead-style side-view boss fights.
Unity 6000.6.10f1, URP 2D, C#. Pixel art at 16 pixels per unit.
Theme: first year of Computer Science at ETH Zürich, full of inside jokes.

## Read first
- README.md (who owns what), docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, and the brief for the workflow in your task:
  docs/workflows/W1-core.md, W2-overworld.md, W3-dialogue-ui.md, W4-combat.md, W5-progression-art-audio.md.
- Search only Assets/_Game/ and docs/. Never search Library/ or other generated folders.

## Hard rules
- Only create or edit files inside the module folder(s) of the workflow named in the task.
  If the task needs a change in another module, STOP and explain what is needed instead.
- Never modify Assets/_Game/Core/Contracts/ unless the task explicitly says "contract change".
- Never edit .unity, .prefab, .asset or .meta files by hand. Write C# and ScriptableObject classes;
  humans wire scenes and create assets in the Editor. Tell the human exactly what to wire.
- Never invent GameObject names, tags, layers or scene names. Use the lists in
  Assets/_Game/Core/Docs/Conventions.md, or ask.
- Modules communicate ONLY via Core contracts: interfaces, GameEvents, ScriptableObjects.
  Never reference a class from another module (except EthGame.Core).
- Do not add packages or change ProjectSettings. Ask the human.

## Code style
- Namespace: EthGame.<Module>  (EthGame.Core, EthGame.Overworld, EthGame.Combat, ...).
- One public class per file; file name == class name (required for MonoBehaviours/ScriptableObjects).
- [SerializeField] private fields, no public mutable fields.
- Tunable data (bosses, items, dialogue, XP tables, attack patterns) lives in ScriptableObjects.
- No singletons except GameManager. No Find*/GetComponent calls inside Update.
- Subscribe to GameEvents in OnEnable, unsubscribe in OnDisable.
- Use the new Input System, not UnityEngine.Input.
- Pure logic goes in plain C# classes with EditMode tests in <Module>/Tests/.

## Working style
- Start in Plan mode: list the files you will create or change and why.
- Keep each change small: one feature per branch.
- At the end, write a short summary: files changed, what the human must do in the Editor,
  and how to test it.
